  "fallback": {
    "text": "Binary search begins with a sorted array and a target. It compares the target with the middle value. If the target is larger, the entire left half, including the midpoint, is discarded; if smaller, the right half is discarded. The low and high bounds move inward, and the interval always contains the target if it is present. The midpoint is only the comparison point, not automatically the answer. The search stops when the midpoint matches the target, returning that index, or when low passes high, meaning the target is absent. Discarding a half is valid only because the array is sorted; in an unsorted array, a matching value could be on the discarded side."
  },
